# ADID framework and adm

- **Follow the communication and workflow rules** in the newest versioned file `docs/ADID_Framework_<ver>.md`. The authoritative identifier is `filename + SHA256` (see installer manifest).

- **SV traceability note:** When emitting `SV` / `Prev_MD5s`, the hash must be derived from a **canonical SV string** (sorted keys + normalized weights + fixed precision). Do not hash arbitrary text; otherwise the MD5 chain becomes meaningless noise.
- **SV anchors (15.3+):** `Prev_MD5s` must reference **`md5_sv_tag` anchors** (canonical SV hashes), not message hashes. Keep `Prev_MD5s` short (typically 1 predecessor; only expand during reverse search) so the chain remains meaningful and navigable.

- **When making declarative updates, running verification, or using rollback/template generation**, use the **adm-exe** skill: **prefer `tools/adm.exe` (Windows) or `tools/adm` (Unix)** when the project has adm installed there-it is a stable copy; tool modifications or errors in source will not break the toolchain. Otherwise use `uv run adm` in a repo that has adm as a dependency.

- Apply updates via XML descriptors under `updates/`; run `--verify-all` on selected roots (e.g. `src tests adid_tests`) for a clean report. Do not restore from git over local work; use `--rollback` and descriptor rollbacks instead.

- **Preferred workflow:** Create template descriptor (`--template all` or per-mode templates like `--template replace`), edit the generated descriptor in `updates/`, then apply (`--apply`). To **replay history** (re-apply descriptors in chronological order): use `--replay-updates [dir] [--until TIMESTAMP] [--limit N]` (use `--dry-run` to list only). Do not write XML descriptors from scratch. Multiple backups per file are by design and an advantage (rollback, traceability; project stays manageable).

